content.text:
{
  "primary_meaning": "God (and specifically Christ) as firm foundation, strength, refuge, and source of salvation/security",
  "primary_basis": [
    "Deuteronomy 32:4, 15, 18, 30-31",
    "2 Samuel 22:2-3 / Psalm 18:2, 31, 46",
    "Psalm 62:2, 6-7",
    "Isaiah 26:4; 28:16; 44:8",
    "1 Corinthians 10:4",
    "Matthew 7:24-25; 16:18",
    "1 Peter 2:4-8 (with Psalm 118:22 and Isaiah 8:14)"
  ],
  "alternatives": [
    {
      "meaning": "Peter (or the Petrine office) as the foundation of the Church",
      "basis": [
        "Matthew 16:18 (Catholic and some patristic readings)"
      ],
      "prominence": "common"
    },
    {
      "meaning": "Peter’s confession of faith (or the content of that confession) as the foundation",
      "basis": [
        "Matthew 16:16-18 (common Protestant reading)"
      ],
      "prominence": "common"
    },
    {
      "meaning": "Christ (or the gospel) as a rock of stumbling / offense to the unbelieving",
      "basis": [
        "Isaiah 8:14",
        "Romans 9:33",
        "1 Peter 2:8"
      ],
      "prominence": "common"
    },
    {
      "meaning": "Believers themselves as living stones built on the Rock/Christ",
      "basis": [
        "1 Peter 2:5",
        "Ephesians 2:20-22"
      ],
      "prominence": "minority"
    }
  ],
  "has_settled_consensus": true,
  "confidence": "high",
  "notes": "Across OT and NT the dominant, near-universal Christian usage treats 'rock' as a divine title and metaphor for God’s/Christ’s reliability and protective strength. The only significant ongoing dispute concerns the precise referent of the 'rock' in Matthew 16:18; that local controversy does not overturn the broader symbolic consensus."
}
